SC BRIEF #216
Thursday, September 24, 2026 | The morning read for supply-chain and logistics operators

Good morning. Some tariff exclusions lapsed, trucks are waiting longer at the San Pedro Bay gates, and our practitioner column tackles supplier scorecards. Five items.

1. Section 301 exclusion decisions: 31 lines dropped
USTR let exclusions lapse on 31 of the 164 product lines it reviewed, mostly consumer goods, while extending the rest. Duties on the dropped lines apply to entries from October 1. Check your HTS list against the notice.

2. Truck turn times at LA and Long Beach creep up
Average truck turn times at the San Pedro Bay terminals rose to 78 minutes in the first half of September, from 68 in August, as import volumes stayed high and one terminal reduced weekend gates. Dual transactions (drop and pick in one visit) are the lever; only 40% of moves are dual.

3. Rail intermodal: flat week
Weekly intermodal originations were flat year over year at 271,000 units. Domestic gains offset international declines again.

4. Air cargo: Q4 outlook firm, rates up
Freight forwarders are quoting transpacific air rates 12% above August levels for October departures, with e-commerce block-space agreements taking roughly a third of freighter capacity. Charter inquiries are up.

5. Supplier scorecards: what the good ones measure
Our practitioner column this week covers supplier scorecards that changed supplier behavior versus the ones that became a quarterly ritual. The short version: measure on-time-in-full, quality escapes, and responsiveness to engineering changes; skip the twenty-metric dashboard. https://scbrief.io/reads/scorecards

